<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ala ad-Din Tekish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1172">1172</a>–<a href="/years/1200">1200</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ala ad-Din Tekish was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> from 1172 to 1200. He freed the state from dependence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> and defeated the last Seljuk sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, expanding into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. His reign turned Khwarazm into a major independent power and laid the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-tekish.jpg" alt="Ala ad-Din Tekish" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circa 1425 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takash,_Sultan_of_Khwarazm,_Congratulated_by_Rashid_al-Din-i_Vatvat,_Yale_University_Art_Gallery,_accession_number_1983-94-10_(Takash_portrait)_Contrast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the west they took Herat around 1175 and contested Khurasan with the Khwarazmshahs, briefly occupying Nishapur after the death of the Khwarazmshah <b>Tekish</b> in 1200.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ala ad-Din Tekish</b> (r. 1172–1200) the dynasty threw off Seljuk overlordship altogether, defeating and killing Toghrul III, the last Seljuk sultan of Iraq, in 1194 and seizing much of western Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iraqi line staggered on through a succession of weak sultans dominated by their atabegs until Toghrul III was killed in battle by the Khwarazm Shah <b>Tekish</b> in 1194, extinguishing the Great Seljuk line entirely.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Tughril III tried to free himself from the Eldiguzids, but he was killed near Ray in 1194 by the Khwarazmshah <b>Tekish</b> of the Khwarazmian Empire, ending Seljuk rule in Iran.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-iraq">Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32,53&amp;year=1186" aria-label="Iran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5914 999 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ala ad-Din Tekish"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6364 1269h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32,53&amp;year=1186">What was here in 1186? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
